WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.599 --> 00:00:03.990 align:start position:0%
 
do you care about politics  or maybe i should rather ask do you care  about politics in your favorite show or  series  regardless whether you answer this  question with yes or no  you may not be aware of how big a deal  thoughtful political writing can be in  storytelling  because the truth is deep down we all do  care how things are being run in the  world  our heroes live in and also by whom  this is especially obvious for stories  where the political system is unfair  or straight out evil and political  structures can give a story world  a level of color and realism that makes  it incredibly easy to engage with  but when i talk about politics i in no  way mean focusing purely on political  maneuvering  after all most of us don't want every  story to be a house of cards  much rather good political writing  usually is subtle  and only throws us small bits and pieces  of the overarching structure of the  society our heroes live in  while modern television has discovered  the value of political intrigue and  action  shonen manga might not exactly be the  first genre that comes to your mind in  this context  and yet one of the reasons one piece has  managed to become as  popular as it is today is the political  aspect oda has increasingly integrated  into the story  in fact one character in particular has  enriched one piece in a number of ways  that might not be obvious at first  glance because  vivi is a wonderful example of political  writing  done right with her introduction to the  story  oda also introduces a proper political  system for the first time  that made the overall story more  entertaining and successfully  distinguished it from other shonen  mangas competing with it over the years  bivy's character arc shows her growing  into a great political leader  and gives us an idea of oda's own  political values  not only does vv trigger important  character and story development  by teaching luffy to become a better  leader himself but she also enhances our  perception of the straw hat crew in  general  by clearly contrasting what makes them  so unique in the one piece universe  and i think that this power of political  writing especially in the subgenre of  shonen manga  is worth taking a closer look at because  betting their adventure in a sense of  realism allows our heroes to break out  of these everyday conventions we're  familiar with  and thus in turn help us escape from our  everyday lives  what makes vivi so different from any  other character that we've met in the  story up to this point  is that her character arc is not the  story of a pirate  but the story of a leader after the  introductory arcs of the east blue  meeting vivi is the beginning of the  first real saga  in the straw heads adventure spanning  over multiple different arcs  getting to know the princess of alabasta  and witnessing her struggle to  free her country from the grasp of one  of the seven warlords of the sea  introduces a conflict on a much larger  scale  than anything we've gotten up to this  point being a compassionate and brave  young woman  that cares deeply for her people it  quickly becomes clear that her task is  not to get physically stronger in order  to take down her political opponent  crocodile  but rather to become a better politician  herself  to do so right from the get-go oda  already equipped her character with a  number of traits that show us her true  potential  as a leader very very early on in the  story even as a kid  vivi already shows a notch for politics  and diplomacy  when slapped by an angry whipple she  keeps her cool  not to cause a major international  incident for her father and country  she also befriends a group of common  kids including the young coza  who later would lead the rebellion in  the civil war staged by crocodile  and in general far from being  authoritative and tempered  vivi fulfills the trope of the kind and  adventurous princess  that is brave and determined to take  action for her country's sake  thus she doesn't hesitate to infiltrate  crocodile's baroque work agency  at the age of only 14 and works her way  up the ladder  over a two-year period in which she even  learns how to fight  further she's also clearly able to deal  with pain  be it physical from being shot in the  shoulder or  psychological from losing egoron so when  she meets luffy  who very quickly comes to acknowledge  her determination she already shows  enormous promise to become a great  leader in the future  however crocodile manipulating her  country into a famine  and ultimately civil war proves to be  too much for her to handle  and she suffers deeply under her  people's hardship  falsely believing that she has to carry  her country's burden all on her own  it takes a quite emotional brawl with  luffy to show her that she needs to  start believing in her friends  and let them share in on her load still  in spite of luffy's speech she once  again tries to end the bloody battle  between the royal army and the rebels  all by herself  and not only does she fail to be hurt  she also fails to stop the bomb  planted by crocodile in the middle of  the city this  is the low point of a character arc  however  luffy defeating crocodile and finally  returning the reign  as well as pel sacrificing himself by  carrying the bomb away from the city  finally held vivi in overcoming her  biggest weakness as a leader  thus in her coming of age speech we  finally see a future queen  that has grown significantly through her  shared adventures with the straw hats  in contrast to the power focused pirate  battles we have seen up to this point  vivi introduces a political level to the  story  that up to this day helps distinguishing  it from other shonen  alabaster and the baroque work saga are  the first  real taste of political intrigue and  conflict in one piece  way before arcs like anna's lobby or  dressrosa that are even more centered  around political themes  with crocodile for the first time ever  we encounter an antagonist that  cannot simply be beaten by pure strength  alone  but rather has to be approached in  multiple steps over multiple arcs  requiring the skills of the entire crew  this brings an entirely new level of  complexity and tension into the story  that not only make the story less  predictable but also requires  more thoughtful and creative action from  our heroes  similarly the political level of  alabasta also enlarges the entire story  world we had known up to this point  for the first time it becomes clear that  the one piece world does  not only consist of small islands with  quiet villages  as we've exclusively seen in the east  blue much rather we learned that many  islands consist of entire kingdoms  with their own political systems that  have existed for hundreds  if not thousands of years this insight  broadens our view of the world  immensely and prepares us for the  overarching conflict with the world  government  thus vivi's character enables important  story development overall  i think it goes even further than this  because she also triggers important  character development for the entire  crew  and especially so for luffy see while  luffy teaches vivi about the importance  of trusting in your friends  and sharing the burden vv in turn  teaches luffy a crucial lesson about  leadership  after leaving little garden vivi  convinces the crew to make a detour for  the severely ill nami and find a doctor  on drum island  and upon arriving at the small village  on the island shore  the villagers led by the future king  dalton surround the ship and back in the  crew to turn around  over the course of this tense situation  vivi is  shot in the shoulder and seeing his  friend being hurt  luffy as he always does starts charging  at the villagers  however vivi stops him in the last  moment and goes down to the villages in  apology  she calls out luffy for his rash  reaction that might have caused nami's  life  and in this moment luffy makes a  graspable leap in his character  development  he bows down himself and asks for help  to save his navigator  the important lesson that vivi taught  luffy here  is that some situations cannot simply be  resolved through fighting alone  some situations call for diplomacy  strategy  or even humility this ultimately helps  luffy to become a better leader himself  and we can actually see his growth from  the scene in later arcs  on sabahodi for instance luffy actually  orders his crew to flee  realizing that fighting against an  admiral is futile at this point  and on jaya he and zoro do not fight  back against bellamy's men  just like shanks did not fight back  against the bandits back when luffy was  a kid  thus luffy has gotten a step closer to  shanks ideal of a great pirate  all through learning from vivi's words  and aside from her personal influence on  luffy and the others  her character benefits us the readers  even more  vivi is a character that is caught in  the gruesome normality of the one piece  world  no matter how hard she tries to take  things into her own hands and  change them with all her might she  doesn't seem able to escape the grasps  of the powerful forces at work in her  country  and while it's easy to confuse her  normality helplessness and frustration  as something of little importance when  looking at it through the lens of  typical shonen stories  what vivi's character actually does for  us is contrasting  how unique and special luffy and his  crew are in this unforgiving system  how they're actually physically and  mentally strong enough to take on these  major forces at work in the one piece  world  this is the true secret of the success  vivi has brought to oda's story  she allows us to widen our focus and  shows us that luffy  zara and ko are indeed not normal  this makes their adventure just the more  exciting as we now know just  how unique they actually are inside this  complex political web  of powerful and influential people being  able to carry out this fundamental  change in his approach to storytelling  in such a seamless way is part of the  genius of oda's writing  subtly integrating such conceptual  layers beneath the main plotline  and thus being able to share a richer  and more vivid world with us readers  is what i found truly thrilling about  one piece as a piece of literature  after all writing a character like vv  takes not only a lot of creativity and  skill  but also a significant portion of  bravery in a genre that otherwise has  been heavily dominated by ever repeating  power struggles  however far from making one piece less  exciting or intense  political characters like vivi actually  enhance and  contrast the protagonists and help bring  out the best in them  so in the end you may think that you  don't care about politics in your  favorite story  until you find out that you actually do  maybe  just not quite in the way that you  expect it to  hey everyone thank you so much for  watching what are your thoughts on vv  and her role in the story  and what character would you like to see  me discuss next  share your thoughts with me in the  comments below if you enjoy one piece  and want to learn more about  storytelling  world building or my thoughts on the  latest one piece chapter make sure to  like and subscribe to become part of the  channel right now  i hope to see you around next time and  until then  stay safe and have a wonderful week

